<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willehad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 745–789</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Willehad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willehad (c. 745–789) was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary from Northumbria who worked among the Frisians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> sent him to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, and in 787 he was consecrated as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. He built the first cathedral there shortly before his death and was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willehad.jpg" alt="Willehad" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bischof_Willehad_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Willehad was born in Northumbria and probably received his education at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> under Ecgbert. He was a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>. After his ordination he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> about the year 766, preaching at Dokkum and in Overijssel, to continue the work of Boniface, whom the Frisians had killed in 754. He barely escaped with his life when Frisians wanted to kill him and withdrew to the area around Utrecht, and he and his companions were threatened again after they destroyed some pagan temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> in 777 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was divided into missionary zones, and the zone between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, called Wigmodia, was given to Willehad. From 780 he preached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> on commission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. In 782 the Saxons under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> rebelled against Charlemagne; Willehad was forced to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, and a number of his assistants and friends were killed. He used the time to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he reported on his work to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i">Pope Adrian I</a>.</p><p>On his return he retired for a time to the monastery of Echternach, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, where he spent two years working in the scriptorium and gathering his missionary team again. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> he preached around the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> his diocese was the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Friesland near the mouth of the Weser, and he chose as his see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, a place first mentioned in documents of 782.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The cathedral he built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> was dedicated in 789, and Anschar later praised it for its beauty. Willehad also built a small church at Blexen, and he died there, at a place that is today part of Nordenham, on 8 November 789. He was buried in the cathedral of Bremen.</p><p>Anschar compiled a life of Willehad, and the preface he wrote for it was considered a masterpiece for its age. In 860 a sick girl from Wege (Weyhe) travelled to his grave and was reportedly cured there by a miracle; the account is the first mention of that village in any historical document.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willehad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charlemagne made it the seat of a bishopric in 787, with the Anglo-Saxon missionary <b>Willehad</b> as first bishop, and after Vikings sacked Hamburg in 845 Archbishop Ansgar moved to Bremen, which became the seat of the archbishops of Hamburg-Bremen, leaders of the mission to Scandinavia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Northumbria. Died in Blexen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.53,8.53&amp;year=773" aria-label="Blexen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4740 324 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Willehad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5190 594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5190 594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.53,8.53&amp;year=773">What was here in 773 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
